An iframe renders the embedded page in its own viewport and never grows to fit that page’s content, so either the parent sets a height or the embedded page reports one.


You ship a comment widget or a signup form in an iframe, and it either clips the content behind a second scrollbar or leaves a block of empty space under a short form. A session replay of a fixed-height embed shows the problem clearly: a user scrolling inside a small inner box on the parent page. A screenshot of the initial state never shows that.


This guide covers each approach in order: fixed heights,` aspect-ratio` , the same-origin shortcut, a secure cross-origin` postMessage` pair, and the` frame-sizing` property that Chrome 154 added, with a fallback. For framework-specific embedding, see[integrating iframes with React](https://blog.openreplay.com/integrating-iframes-with-react--a-comprehensive-guide/) .


## Key Takeaways


- Across origins, the parent cannot read the iframe’s document, so the embedded page must post its height with` window.postMessage()` to an explicit target origin.
- A resize listener should act only after it checks` event.origin` against an allowlist, matches` event.source` to the iframe’s` contentWindow` , and validates the height as a finite, non-negative number.
- ResizeObserver on` document.documentElement` catches height changes from images, fonts and reflow, which MutationObserver misses.
- Chrome 154 ships` frame-sizing: content-height` , which needs a` responsive-embedded-sizing` meta tag in the embedded page’s initial HTML. Firefox and Safari don’t support it.
- Wrap` frame-sizing` in` @supports` and keep the` postMessage` script running for browsers without it.


